  "answer_text": "The word **hosanna** (pronounced *ho-ZAN-uh*) is an exclamation of praise, adoration, or joy, primarily used in Jewish and Christian liturgies. \n\nWhile it is commonly used today as a shout of praise (similar to \"Hallelujah\"), its literal, historical definition is actually a plea for salvation.\n\n### 1. Etymology and Literal Meaning\nThe word comes from the Hebrew phrase *hoshiah-na* (\u05d4\u05d5\u05b9\u05e9\u05b4\u05c1\u05d9\u05e2\u05b8\u05d4\u05e0\u05b8\u05bc\u05d0), which translates to **\"Save, please!\"** or **\"Save, we pray!\"**\n*   **Hoshi'a** means \"to save\" or \"deliver\".\n*   **Na** is a particle used to express a polite request, similar to \"please\" or \"I pray\".\n\nOver time, as the word passed from Hebrew into Aramaic, Greek (*h\u014dsanna*), Latin (*osanna*), and eventually English, its usage shifted from a desperate cry for help into a celebratory chant.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Meaning in Judaism\nIn Jewish tradition, *hosanna* (or *hoshana*) remains closely tied to its original meaning as an appeal for God\u2019s help and deliverance. \n*   It is most notably used during the holiday of **Sukkot** (the Feast of Tabernacles), during which daily prayers called *Hoshanot* are recited while holding the Four Species (branches of palm, myrtle, willow, and a citron fruit) and marching around the synagogue. \n*   The seventh day of the festival is known as *Hoshana Rabbah* (\"Great Hosanna\" or \"Great Salvation\").\n\n---\n\n### 3. Meaning in Christianity\nIn Christian tradition, *hosanna* is primarily understood as a shout of worship, triumph, and praise. \n*   **The Triumphal Entry:** Its most famous biblical usage occurs during Jesus\u2019 entry into Jerusalem on what is now celebrated as Palm Sunday. As Jesus rode in on a donkey, crowds waved palm branches and shouted: *\"Hosanna to the Son of David! Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord! Hosanna in the highest!\"* (Matthew 21:9).\n*   **The Dual Meaning on Palm Sunday:** In this context, the crowd was shouting both a prayer and a proclamation. They were crying *\"Save us, please!\"* (recognizing Jesus as the Messiah who had come to rescue them) while simultaneously cheering *\"Hooray for salvation!\"* \n*   **Modern Liturgy:** Today, Christians sing \"Hosanna\" in hymns and during the *Sanctus* (\"Holy, Holy, Holy\") portion of many traditional church services to worship Jesus as the Savior.",
